# platform.linux.sdl

mm.display and mm.touch over SDL2, in one module and bound twice. SDL has one
video subsystem, one window, one event queue, and shared state between drawing
and input; two modules could not own that between them.

It exists for one thing the DRM provider cannot do: run in a window beside the
editor. DRM needs DRM master and a compositor holds it, so anything visual there
means leaving the session for a virtual terminal.

It names a library and is still not a lib. wrapper. Its public dependency is the
project interface module, not a foreign library API, which is the same ground on
which docs/modules-c++20.mdy already exempts a provider over a bridge ABI.

The window is the display provider's. Touch reports the pointer while the left
button is held, and answers Unsupported when no window exists, rather than
opening a second one.

# Unattended capture

The provider can capture an application's screens with no one at the window.
Every control is an environment variable read when the display or touch
initializes, so a program that sets none behaves exactly as before.

Opening fence
MM_SDL_DISPLAY_SIZE=WxH      the logical size, over the map's; the generic
                             boards register no map override reader, so
                             MM_LINUX_DEVICE_MAP cannot set it there
MM_SCREENSHOT_DIR=DIR        frames go here as binary PPMs, frame-NNNNNN.ppm
                             numbered by refresh; capture is off when unset
MM_SCREENSHOT_EVERY=N        every Nth refresh, default 1; 0 writes no
                             periodic frame, leaving F12 and script shots
MM_SCREENSHOT_LIMIT=N        at most N periodic frames, default 1000
MM_TOUCH_SCRIPT=FILE         timed touch steps that replace the pointer
SDL_VIDEODRIVER=offscreen    SDL's own: no window at all, for CI
Closing fence

A PPM is written beside its name and renamed over it, the method
platform.linux.lcd's MM_LCD_SNAPSHOT uses, so a reader never sees half a
frame. F12 writes shot-NNNNNN.ppm. Without MM_SCREENSHOT_DIR, F12 and script
shots go to the current directory.

A touch script has one step per line; # starts a comment.

Opening fence
wait-refresh N     until N more frames have been presented
wait-ms N          until N milliseconds have passed
tap X Y            press at (X, Y) for one read, then release for one
hold X Y N         press for N reads, then release for one
shot NAME          write the current frame as NAME.ppm
exit               end the run as closing the window does
Closing fence

Coordinates are logical pixels and must lie inside the display. A press spans
whole reads, so an application acting on the press edge sees one press and
one release. An application that debounces a short release as a held touch,
as the apps.cpp touch applications do for 35 ms, needs a wait-ms after each
tap. exit makes touch read answer TransportError, which applications treat as
the end of the run. A script that cannot be read or holds a malformed line
fails touch initialization with TransportError, naming the file and line on
standard error, rather than falling back to the pointer silently.

tests/boards/sdl-linux-aarch64 runs both headless: the dumped frames equal
what was drawn, pixel for pixel, at MM_SCREENSHOT_EVERY=2 and 0, and a script
of taps, a hold, a shot, and exit produces exactly the expected reads.
